#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129905} :activity/id #uuid "70c09bba-cfb5-5906-81b1-66e614d9a1e1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049384 :topic/id #uuid "f08caa4e-a236-53c4-a667-fe4274640c5e" :topic/math-academy-id 2004} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130330 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the function $y=\\dfrac 1 x$ below.\n\n![](images/cc/cc9593860b0c134d0d99635b37eeac4f16a712a7af9e1c6474fcf1f885e8451e.png)\n\nThe function has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ There is also a discontinuity in the function at $x=0,$ since we cannot draw the points on the curve without taking our pen off the paper to cross the asymptote.\n\nSo, we say that $y = \\dfrac 1 x$ has a **discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote** at $x=0.$ Discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes are also called **infinite discontinuities**.\n\nNotice that here, both one-sided limits are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) =-\\infty\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac 1 x \\right)=\\infty.\n$$\n\nIn general, a function $y=f(x)$ has a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=c$ if either one-sided limit is infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c^-} f(x) =\\pm \\infty\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to c^+} f(x) =\\pm \\infty.\n$$\n\nNote that we only need a single limit to become infinite to conclude that there is an infinite discontinuity at $x=c.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ce83f36f-d7f8-546f-b887-1e6818a75bfb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2595 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "545e237d-8d82-5fad-8893-b53c598fe510" :step/math-academy-id 12112 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129906}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128903 :question/id #uuid "001529aa-c97a-5d69-82fb-cd4319a4dd7d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3585"} :knowled